---
import ArticleLayout from "@layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import FlairArt from "@components/art/flair.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Flare"
  description="A private Android app that turns live English speech into offline Markdown notes."
  subtitle="Private, offline Markdown notes from your voice."
  eyebrow="2026 · Building / Flowforest"
>
  <FlairArt slot="art" />

  <section>
    <h2>Live transcription, kept on your phone</h2>
    <p>
      Flare is an Android voice-to-Markdown app built by Flowforest. It
      transcribes English speech live and entirely on-device with Moonshine
      Small Streaming. The model is bundled, so the app works offline with no
      account, download at runtime or internet permission.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>A note that fits your existing system</h2>
    <p>
      Choose an optional tag, record, pause or resume, then save or discard.
      Flare writes plain Markdown with tags and configurable YAML front matter.
      Notes live in app storage and can be mirrored to a chosen folder through
      Android’s folder picker, ready for an Obsidian vault or another sync tool.
      Flare itself does not provide sync or import edits from elsewhere.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Capture without creating another data trail</h2>
    <p>
      Audio stays in memory for transcription and is not saved as a recording.
      Flare has no account, ads, analytics or crash-reporting service. Saved
      notes can be read, shared and deleted in the app; deleting an app note
      does not delete an exported copy. The build uses Kotlin, Jetpack Compose,
      coroutines, Flow, Hilt and Android’s Storage Access Framework.
    </p>
  </section>
  <aside class="project-status">
    In development · The visual is a portfolio interface study, not a production
    capture. The project repository does not currently contain shareable app
    screenshots; this page will be updated when real captures are available.
  </aside>
